#{[17592186308593 :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=4>0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, we have a spiral that is traversed toward infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable spiral (source)}.$"] [17592186308594 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)-3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =-1\\pm 3\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}."] [17592186308594 :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=-1<0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, we have a spiral that is traversed toward the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable spiral (sink)}.$"] [17592186308595 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=4x(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =\\pm 4\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}."] [17592186308595 :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{0\\cdot t}=1$ for all $t.$ Thus, we have an ellipse that curves around the origin. \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a center}.$"] [17592186308596 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=5x(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =\\pm 5\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}."] [17592186308596 :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{0\\cdot t}=1$ for all $t.$ Thus, we have an ellipse that curves around the origin. \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a center}.$"] [17592186308597 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=8x(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =\\pm 4\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}."] [17592186308597 :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{0\\cdot t}=1$ for all $t.$ Thus, we have an ellipse that curves around the origin. \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a center}.$"] [17592186308598 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=12x(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =\\pm 6\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}."] [17592186308598 :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{0\\cdot t}=1$ for all $t.$ Thus, we have an ellipse that curves around the origin. \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a center}.$"] [17592186310939 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186309755] [17592186311122 :answer-field/choices #edb/ref 17592186308780] [17592186311122 :answer-field/choices #edb/ref 17592186309522] [17592186311122 :answer-field/choices #edb/ref 17592186309829] [17592186311122 :answer-field/choices #edb/ref 17592186310026] [17592186311122 :answer-field/choices #edb/ref 17592186310249] [17592186311228 :answer-field/choices #edb/ref 17592186309306] [17592186311228 :answer-field/choices #edb/ref 17592186309339] [17592186311228 :answer-field/choices #edb/ref 17592186309472] [17592186311228 :answer-field/choices #edb/ref 17592186310236] [17592186311228 :answer-field/choices #edb/ref 17592186310565] [17592186311237 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186310561] [17592186311317 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186309119] [17592186311416 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186309095] [17592186311807 :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4xy+3x\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form. This gives\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-(4xy+3x),\\,Q(x,y)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-4}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-5}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $x$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-7}},\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}.\n$$"] [17592186311852 :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3y-2\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form. This gives\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2-3y,\\,Q(x,y)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-4}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-5}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $x$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-7}},\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}.\n$$"] [17592186311855 :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n3x\\,{y}^{′}=-6y-4{x}^{2}\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form. This gives\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+6y,\\,Q(x,y)=3x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-4}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-5}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $x$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-7}},\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}.\n$$"] [17592186311873 :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}{y}^{′}=-x{y}^{3}-x,\\,x>0\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form. This gives\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=x({y}^{3}+1),\\,Q(x,y)={y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-4}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-5}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $y$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-7}},\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}.\n$$"] [17592186319202 :question/worked-solution "We are given that $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=1$ into the solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\ 1 & =Asin\\,(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ 1 & =Asin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\ 1 & =A(1) \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186321016 :question/worked-solution "We are given that $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)=\\sqrt{2}$ into the solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =Acos\\,(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =A\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186321017 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A{e}^{3x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(2)=5.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(2)=5$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{3x} \\\\ 5 & =A{e}^{3\\cdot 2} \\\\ 5 & =A{e}^{6} \\\\ \\frac{5}{{e}^{6}} & =A \\\\ A & =\\frac{5}{{e}^{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186321018 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A{e}^{-4x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(1)=2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(1)=2$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-4x} \\\\ 2 & =A{e}^{-4\\cdot 1} \\\\ 2 & =A{e}^{-4} \\\\ 2{e}^{4} & =A \\\\ A & =2{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
